WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.720
NeMo Helix is available on
GitHub under Apache 2.0.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.440
You can install it with one command and
then run nemo setup to start it up locally.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.440
From here on, I can work in a coding
assistant, Claude Code in this case.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.630
I ask it to register our incident
agent with Helix and deploy it.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.940
The agent is described as a YAML file.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.080
It's running in NeMo Studio.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.220
The included web UI shows it as healthy.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.520
Now, let's look at our example.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.400
We have a Slack message from an engineer.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.720
The scheduler on pod-c is lagging,
but nothing is broken for users yet.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.400
The agent's triage step runs on
Nemotron 3 Ultra with Meridian's notes

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:50.740
in its prompt.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.150
It pages the on-call engineer.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.320
This issue isn't really urgent and
could have waited until the morning.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.440
In Studio, you can see the
three steps of that run.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.300
Only the first one needs
Meridian's knowledge.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.550
Next, we send the same message to the
same agent with that one step moved

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.180
to a fine-tuned Qwen3.5-4B.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.140
It opens a ticket for the morning,
which is the right decision.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.110
Let's see how we fine-tuned
the fast decision model.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.680
We'll start with creating the
training data using Data Designer.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.900
Here's the actual recipe file.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.560
At the top are the two models it uses.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.530
First, it picks the
answer for each example,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.500
which team owns the incident
and what to do about it.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.060
Then, a model writes a realistic
incident report to match,

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.220
without naming the answer.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.100
A second model checks every example.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.640
Now I ask Claude Code to run it in Helix.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.260
It starts generating incidents.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.960
Each example comes back
with a quality score.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.870
Here's what the data looks like.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.660
The answer and the
incident written for it.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.880
Slack messages, pager alerts, tickets.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.690
We throw out examples like this
one that give the answer away,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.280
so the model is learning
without being given the answers.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.260
This is the dataset we trained on.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.780
We turn the dataset into questions
and answers for the model.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.920
And upload them to Helix.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:49.040
Then, Claude Code starts the fine-tuning
job with the Helix post-training plugin.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.420
The plugin can train with NeMo
Automodel, NeMo RL, or Unsloth.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:59.000
We used Unsloth because this is
a small fine-tune on one GPU.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.640
Here's the finished training run.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.560
Helix serves the original model and
the fine-tuned one side-by-side.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.040
We ask both the same question.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.560
The original model doesn't pick an action.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.780
The fine-tuned one picks the ticket.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.740
With the fine-tuned model in the
agent, the same message gets a ticket.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.920
One message isn't proof, so we run an
evaluation with the Helix Evals plugin.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:44.080
Every version of the agent is scored
here on the same test incidents.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.480
You can open any one of them.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.600
Here's one side-by-side.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.070
The fine-tuned agent opens a ticket.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.380
The big model pages on-call.
